  "title": "The 102 Million Console Nintendo Walked Away From",
  "name_card": "Ken Kutaragi",
  "name_sub": "you, the PlayStation builder",
  "description": "You are Ken Kutaragi. Your PlayStation sold over 102 million units. And it began as a side room for Nintendo.\n\nSources:\n- Wikipedia: PlayStation (console): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PlayStation_(console)\n- Wikipedia: Super NES CD-ROM: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_NES_CD-ROM\n- Wikipedia: Ken Kutaragi: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ken_Kutaragi\n\nFrom the cited pages: Sony and Nintendo began a CD-ROM add-on project for the Super NES in 1988; Kutaragi secretly designed the Super NES sound chip after watching his daughter play a Famicom and was nearly fired; at the 1991 Consumer Electronics Show Sony announced the Play Station with Nintendo, and the next day Nintendo revealed a partnership with Philips; in June 1992 Sony chairman Norio Ohga kept Kutaragi's project alive despite opposition from older executives; the PlayStation launched in Japan on 3 December 1994 and sold 100,000 units on the first day; it launched in North America on 9 September 1995 at $299; by early 1996 it had grossed $2 billion from hardware and software; it was the first computer entertainment platform to ship over 100 million units, with 102.49 million sold in total. This is a retelling of public history, not company news. Not financial advice.\n\n#shorts #playstation #sony #nintendo #howtheymakemoney",
